          id: `l20-welcome`,
          type: `welcome`,
          guideText: `{name}. Here we are — the final lesson of our whole journey together, and it's the most important one, because it's entirely about you. Across nineteen lessons, you've thought clearly about some of the deepest questions human beings have ever asked: what is real, how we know anything, what makes actions right or wrong, what the self is, what consciousness is, whether there's a God, what beauty is, how to handle the future, and how to live well in a finite life. You've learned to spot bad arguments and manipulation. You've learned to steelman views you disagree with, to hold strong convictions with genuine humility, to tell sincere wisdom from grift in every costume it wears. You've built, lesson by lesson, an extraordinary toolkit for thinking and living. Today, we do something with all of it: we weave it together into your own personal philosophy. Now, "personal philosophy" might sound grand, but it just means this: an examined, coherent way of seeing the world and living your life that is genuinely YOURS — not borrowed unthinkingly from others, not sold to you, not absorbed by accident, but chosen and built by you, with your own clear thinking. Almost everyone has a philosophy of life whether they know it or not; the difference is whether it's one you stumbled into or one you actually examined and made your own. Today you become the author. This is your graduation from this band, and you've more than earned it. So let's gather everything you've learned and use it to build something that will serve you for the rest of your life: a way of thinking and living that is clear, kind, brave, hard to fool, open to wonder, and unmistakably yours. Let's write your philosophy.`,
          headline: `Writing Your Personal Philosophy`,
          subtitle: `The capstone. Weave everything you've learned into a way of seeing and living that is truly your own. Build it.`,
