              audioPrompt: `Let's take inventory of the thinking tools you've built, {name}, because they form the foundation of your personal philosophy: the how of clear thinking, before the what you believe. You learned to examine arguments: to ask whether a conclusion actually follows from its premises, to spot logical fallacies, to distinguish a clever-sounding argument from a genuinely sound one. You learned about knowledge and evidence: that good beliefs are proportioned to the evidence, that extraordinary claims need extraordinary support, and the priceless concept of unfalsifiability, recognizing claims built so that nothing could ever disprove them. You learned intellectual humility: holding your views with confidence while staying genuinely open to being wrong, and matching your certainty to your actual evidence rather than to how loud or scared you feel. You learned, maybe most valuably, the art of steelmanning: understanding views you disagree with at their strongest, not their weakest, which makes you both fairer and far harder to fool. You practiced evenhandedness on the most contested questions, politics, religion, ethics, the future, holding your own convictions while treating those who differ as sincere, thoughtful people rather than fools or villains. And running through everything, you built the single most important protective skill of this entire band: the ability to detect manipulation in every costume it wears. The fingerprints are the same whether they show up in a cult, a scam, a conspiracy theory, a political movement, a wellness grift, or a spiritual predator: isolation from people who'd question it, fear-based control, an authority placed beyond all question, demands for money, claims built to be unfalsifiable so no evidence could ever count against them. Once you can see that pattern, you can't be trapped by it, no matter what language it's dressed in. Notice what all these tools have in common: they're about thinking for yourself, clearly and honestly, instead of letting others do your thinking for you. That's the spine of any personal philosophy worth having. A philosophy isn't a fixed set of conclusions you memorize; it's first and foremost a way of thinking: a commitment to examining your beliefs, following evidence and good reasoning, staying humble and curious, being fair to other views, and refusing to be manipulated. If you carry nothing else from this whole band, carry this: you now know how to think well, which means that even as the world changes and you meet questions we never covered, you have the tools to think them through yourself. That's worth more than any particular set of answers, because answers can go out of date, but the ability to think clearly never does. Now let's build the rest of the house on top.`,
